  "query_preview": "..\n\n\nKEY MESSAGES:\n\n - Colder temperatures and areas of accumulating snow will create\n   slick travel conditions today through Saturday, especially \n   over mountain passes.\n\n - A southwesterly system on Sunday will bring additional light \n   snow accumulations.\n\n - A warming and drying trend will arrive by the middle of next \n   week.\n\nA secondary low-pressure system digging into the region from \nCanada in northwesterly flow remains the primary driver for our \nincoming weather. As this system pushes into the Northern Rockies,\ncolder air filtering in will transition precipitation to \naccumulating snow today into Saturday. This snowfall will create \nslick roadways particularly during the overnight and early morning\nhours in portions of western Montana and particularly near the \nDivide over key mountain routes like Marias, MacDonald, and \nHomestake Passes.\n\nOn Sunday, the weather pattern shifts as a southwesterly short \nwave trough approaches the region. This system will feature \noverrunning moisture, where relatively warmer, moist air from the \nsouthwest rides up and over the colder air already entrenched at \nthe surface across the Northern Rockies. This setup will keep \nlight snow in the forecast through Sunday, with minor additional \naccumulations largely focused across higher terrain and \nsouthwesterly upslope regions.\n\nBy the middle of next week, high pressure will build across the \nIntermountain West. This pattern change will shut off the active \nmoisture feed and bring a noticeable warming and drying trend to \nwestern Montana and north-central Idaho.",
